  "worked_solution": "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
